------------------------------------------------------------------------
(optional) What information can you provide about your submission that
will help your grader to grade it in the most favorable light? In
particular, what bugs are in your submission?

The instruction for building mywc.s was that "use the DATA section 
for any global variables that were explicitly initialized to zero"
but iChar was not explicitly initialized to zero. Since it was 
not initialized and is implicitly zero, I put it in BSS.

Also, I put an extra statement test file even though the first one 
covered every statement.

I also used more registers to optimize under 1.26 since just the 
three optimizations gave me 1.33
------------------------------------------------------------------------
What is your mywc program test plan?

Boundary tests:
mywcBound1.txt: This file is empty (0 bytes). It tests the boundary 
condition of the smallest possible input. getchar() returns EOF 
immediately so the while loop on line 30 is never entered. iInWord is 
FALSE so the condition on line 52 is false. The program prints all 
zeros.

mywcBound2.txt: This file contains a single newline character. 
It tests the smallest input that produces a non-zero line count. The 
while loop on line 30 executes one iteration. isspace returns true on 
line 34, but iInWord is FALSE so line 36 is false and lines 38-39 are 
not executed. Line 48 is true so line 49 increments lLineCount.

mywcBound3.txt: This file contains a single character 'a' with no 
trailing newline. It tests the smallest possible word and the boundary
where EOF arrives while in a word. isspace is false on line 34, so the
else on line 42 is taken, and since iInWord is FALSE, line 44 is true 
and line 45 sets iInWord to TRUE. After EOF, line 52 is true so line 
53 counts the last word. 

mywcBound4.txt: This file contains a single space character. It tests 
the boundary of whitespace-only input with no words. isspace is true 
on line 34, but iInWord is FALSE so line 36 is false and lines 38-39 
are not executed. No words are counted.

mywcBound5.txt: This file contains "hello\n" — one word followed by a 
newline. It tests a single complete line containing one word. The word
ends when the newline (whitespace) is read, causing lines 36, 38-39 to
execute. Line 48 is true for the newline so line 49 executes. After 
EOF, iInWord is FALSE so line 52 is false and line 53 is not executed.

mywcBound6.txt: This file contains a single tab character (0x09). It 
tests the boundary condition that isspace correctly identifies tab as 
whitespace, not just space and newline characters. isspace returns 
true on line 34, but iInWord is FALSE so lines 38-39 are skipped. 
Line 48 is false so line 49 is not executed.

mywcBound7.txt: This file contains "   hi   \n" — a word surrounded by
leading and trailing spaces. It tests the boundary of consecutive 
whitespace before and after a word. Leading spaces hit line 34 true 
and line 36 false (not in a word). Trailing spaces after the word hit 
line 36 true, executing lines 38-39 (ending the word). Only the first 
trailing space triggers lines 38-39; subsequent trailing spaces hit 
line 36 false.

mywcBound8.txt: This file contains "hi there\n": two words separated 
by a space on one line. It tests the minimal multi-word input and the 
transition from one word through whitespace into a second word.

Statement tests:
mywcStmt1.txt: This file contains "hi  there\nhello" (two spaces 
between "hi" and "there", a newline, then "hello" with no trailing 
newline). This causes the computer to execute: line 30 (loop iterates 
for each of 15 characters plus EOF exit); line 32 (lCharCount++ for 
each of 15 characters); line 34 true for ' ', ' ', and '\n' (isspace 
returns true), and line 34 false for all letters (else branch on line 
42 taken); line 36 true when the first space is read after "hi" 
(iInWord is TRUE), causing lines 38-39 to execute (ending a word); 
line 36 false when the second space is read (iInWord is already 
FALSE), so lines 38-39 are skipped; line 44 true when 't' is read 
after the spaces and when 'h' is read after '\n' (iInWord is FALSE, 
starting a new word), causing line 45 to execute; line 44 false when 
'i','h','e','r','e','e','l','l','o' are read (iInWord is already 
TRUE); line 48 true when '\n' is read, causing line 49 to execute; 
line 48 false for all non-newline characters; line 52 true after EOF 
(iInWord is TRUE because "hello" has no trailing whitespace), causing 
line 53 to execute; line 55 (printf) and line 56 (return 0) always 
execute. This single file causes every line of mywc.c to be executed 
at least once.

mywcStmt2.txt: This file contains "abc\tdef  ghi\njkl" (a tab between 
"abc" and "def", two spaces between "def" and "ghi", a newline, then 
"jkl" with no trailing newline). This provides additional statement 
coverage with a different input pattern. This causes the computer to 
execute all the same lines as mywcStmt1.txt: line 30 (loop), line 32 
(lCharCount++), line 34 true for tab, spaces, and newline, line 34 
false for letters, line 36 true when tab is read after "abc" and when 
first space is read after "def" and when newline is read after "ghi" 
(lines 38-39 execute), line 36 false when second space is read (lines 
38-39 skipped), lines 44-45 execute when starting each word, line 44 
false in the middle of each word, lines 48-49 execute for the newline,
line 48 false for non-newlines, line 52 true at EOF (in word "jkl"), 
line 53 executes, lines 55-56 execute. The use of a tab character 
confirms that the isspace check on line 34 correctly handles all 
whitespace types, not just spaces and newlines.

Stress tests:
mywcStress1.txt: This file was generated by a C program (teststress.c) 
that produces pseudo-random characters using rand() seeded with 
srand(time(NULL)). The generated characters are restricted to 0x09 
(tab), 0x0A (newline), and 0x20 through 0x7E (printable ASCII). The 
file contains at most 50000 characters and at most 1000 lines. The 
random input stresses the program with unpredictable sequences of 
whitespace and non-whitespace characters, creating patterns of word 
lengths, line lengths, and whitespace runs that human-generated input 
would be unlikely to produce.

mywcStress2.txt: This file was generated by the same C program 
(teststress.c) with a different random seed, producing a different 
pattern of characters. The same character restrictions apply (0x09, 
0x0A, 0x20-0x7E) and the same size limits (at most 50000 characters, 
at most 1000 lines). Having a second randomly generated file provides 
additional stress testing confidence with an independent random 
sequence.

